{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdYo72WxK8mokQxFCm3v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A site operator who wants to set a preference about AI training currently has to identify each provider's crawler and maintain a separate robots.txt rule for it. OpenAI's October 30 announcement presents a shared signal as a way to cut that work, and says it will honor the signal for content use in foundational generative AI training.\n\nThe claim that a shared signal reduces the work is OpenAI's own, and the announcement gives no figures or examples of how much. It also describes a plan. OpenAI says it aims to implement the signal in the coming months and will share details when it does, so no one can yet check how the signal behaves in practice. The effort is voluntary, and the article does not say which other providers have agreed to honor the signal. It says only that OpenAI is joining other participants in the IETF's AI Preferences working group in the announcement, and that it encourages other AI providers to honor the signal as well.\n\nOne point is already settled by OpenAI's statement. The new signal does not replace the controls the company already offers through robots.txt, and those existing crawler controls will continue to apply and be respected. An operator who has written rules for OpenAI's crawlers therefore has no stated reason to remove them. My reading is that the simplification arrives only once enough providers honor the common signal that per-crawler rules become unnecessary, and the article does not say when, or whether, that will happen."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":780,"cache_read_input_tokens":2724,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":780},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_